WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.142
Choosing a database starts with the operations
the application must perform. A store

00:00:05.142 --> 00:00:10.650
provides more than persistence: it can supply
indexes, concurrency control, analysis close

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.914
to data and a boundary separating application
code from storage management. Consider a

00:00:15.914 --> 00:00:21.423
workshop service that reserves machines,
searches project descriptions and serves
tutorial

00:00:21.423 --> 00:00:22.158
photographs.

00:00:22.158 --> 00:00:27.507
A reservation changes a scarce resource under
concurrency. A search retrieves documents by

00:00:27.507 --> 00:00:32.976
words rather than by a known identifier. A
photograph is a large object usually fetched as
a

00:00:32.976 --> 00:00:38.325
whole. These operations share a user interface,
but they ask storage to do different work.

00:00:38.325 --> 00:00:43.597
Listing them explicitly is more informative than
beginning with a contest between SQL and

00:00:43.597 --> 00:00:44.367
NoSQL labels.

00:00:44.367 --> 00:00:49.819
For each operation, write down its lookup key,
result size, update frequency and required

00:00:49.819 --> 00:00:55.148
invariant. A machine cannot have two overlapping
confirmed bookings. Search results may

00:00:55.148 --> 00:01:00.599
tolerate a brief indexing delay, while
confirmation cannot depend on an arbitrarily
stale

00:01:00.599 --> 00:01:01.763
availability count.

00:01:01.867 --> 00:01:06.602
Images may be immutable after publication,
allowing long-lived delivery caches. This

00:01:06.602 --> 00:01:11.619
exercise can justify a relational source of
truth for reservations and object storage for

00:01:11.619 --> 00:01:16.466
media without automatically requiring a separate
search service. The first question is

00:01:16.466 --> 00:01:20.243
whether the simpler existing store can meet the
search requirement.

00:01:20.367 --> 00:01:26.118
Every extra component introduces another update
path, recovery procedure and opportunity for

00:01:26.118 --> 00:01:28.119
two representations to disagree.

00:01:28.367 --> 00:01:33.716
A checkout using a gift card changes several
related facts: the remaining balance, an order

00:01:33.716 --> 00:01:39.124
and its line items. Suppose the balance is
deducted but the process crashes before
inserting

00:01:39.124 --> 00:01:44.003
the order details. The customer has lost
purchasing power without a complete order.

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:48.822
A database transaction groups the changes so
they commit together or are rolled back

00:01:48.822 --> 00:01:54.043
together. This is atomicity. Draw a boundary
around the balance update, order insertion and

00:01:54.043 --> 00:01:58.117
line-item insertion; the boundary expresses the
all-or-nothing outcome.

00:01:58.117 --> 00:02:03.124
It does not by itself explain what happens when
another checkout reads and changes the same

00:02:03.124 --> 00:02:08.075
balance concurrently. That requires considering
isolation and the update protocol as well.

00:02:08.117 --> 00:02:12.718
Let two requests each read a balance of one
hundred and each attempt to spend eighty. If

00:02:12.718 --> 00:02:17.424
both independently decide that funds are
sufficient and overwrite the balance with
twenty,

00:02:17.424 --> 00:02:20.614
the system can create two orders while deducting
only eighty.

00:02:20.617 --> 00:02:25.905
A safer approach performs an atomic conditional
decrement that succeeds only if sufficient

00:02:25.905 --> 00:02:30.958
balance remains, checks the affected-row result,
and creates the order within the same

00:02:30.958 --> 00:02:36.011
transaction. The losing request reports
insufficient funds. Appropriate row locking or

00:02:36.011 --> 00:02:39.361
serializable execution can provide other valid
protocols.

00:02:39.361 --> 00:02:44.268
If the order insertion then fails, the
successful decrement rolls back. Trace both the

00:02:44.268 --> 00:02:48.948
competing-request path and the mid-transaction
failure; they are distinct tests of

00:02:48.948 --> 00:02:49.632
correctness.

00:02:49.861 --> 00:02:55.405
Operational requests usually touch a small part
of a dataset: retrieve an order, update a

00:02:55.405 --> 00:03:00.949
delivery status or reserve one seat. Analytical
questions often inspect a large fraction:

00:03:00.949 --> 00:03:05.122
over several years, which districts had the
longest delivery times?

00:03:05.361 --> 00:03:10.601
The first workload values quick individual
decisions under concurrency. The second may

00:03:10.601 --> 00:03:16.023
perform a few expensive scans and aggregations.
Both can use tables and SQL, so the query

00:03:16.023 --> 00:03:19.618
language alone does not identify the right
physical layout.

00:03:19.861 --> 00:03:25.195
A system optimized for thousands of tiny updates
is not automatically optimized for a broad

00:03:25.195 --> 00:03:30.470
historical scan. Begin by tracing how much data
the operation must examine and how much it

00:03:30.470 --> 00:03:32.698
ultimately returns to the application.

00:03:32.861 --> 00:03:37.855
Suppose an analytical query needs district,
duration and date from millions of delivery

00:03:37.855 --> 00:03:43.137
records, but not addresses, notes or package
descriptions. A column-oriented layout can read

00:03:43.137 --> 00:03:48.074
the relevant columns together and compress
repeated values. The engine groups rows and

00:03:48.074 --> 00:03:53.240
computes averages close to storage, returning a
small result instead of shipping every raw

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:54.561
event over the network.

00:03:54.561 --> 00:04:00.015
The diagram should show many stored rows
becoming a few grouped results. This explains
the

00:04:00.015 --> 00:04:05.530
benefit more clearly than saying that analytics
needs a faster database. Parallel execution

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:10.802
can divide large scans, but layout, compression,
pruning and query shape also influence

00:04:10.802 --> 00:04:16.014
performance; not every analytical query requires
a cluster or avoids indexes entirely.
